@database "tool_script"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: tool_script.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Tool Script"
@{h1}Tool Script

An agent that chains tools -- read a document, reshape it, post it -- pays
for every hop: each result enters its context, and it types the values
again as the next call's arguments, which is slow, costs tokens and now and
then garbles a value. With this plugin the agent writes one small Python
script instead. The script calls the agent's other tools on the server, one
after the other; their results stay in the script, and only what the script
hands back as @{tt}result@{utt} reaches the model. A script can call only
what the agent itself may call.

The plugin has no panel. You see a script at work in the chat's status
line: which call it is on, and what it logs.

@{code}
For agents and developers
  @{"  The run_script tool  " link tool}
  @{"  What a script may call  " link calls}
  @{"  The script language  " link language}
  @{"  Limits  " link limits}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node tool "The run_script tool"
@{h1}The run_script tool

The plugin gives an agent one tool, @{tt}<instance>_run_script@{utt}
(@{tt}wf_pipe_run_script@{utt} for the reference agent). It runs the script
it is given, once, in a fresh sandbox: nothing is kept from one script to the
next.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
script | the Python source; required, an empty one is refused
timeout | seconds for this run; at most the server's @{tt}timeout@{utt} (120), which is also what applies without it or with 0; a negative, non-numeric, infinite or NaN value is refused
dry_run | true: only check the syntax and list the tools the script names; nothing runs
@{body}

@{h2}What a script has

@{table}
Name | Does
call_tool(name, **params) | calls a tool by the exact name from the agent's tool list and returns its result as plain data
log(text) | a progress line: shown in the chat's status line and returned in @{tt}log@{utt}
parse_json(text) | turns JSON text into data -- tools often return JSON as a string, and there is no json module; bad JSON raises ToolCallError
ToolCallError | what a failed call raises; catch it with @{tt}except ToolCallError:@{utt}
result | not given, but read at the end: whatever the script assigns to it is the answer
@{body}

A call fails with ToolCallError when the tool answers
@{tt}{"status": "error", ...}@{utt} or @{tt}{"error": ...}@{utt} without a
status, when its parameters do not fit its schema, when the agent may not
call it, when a tool hook blocks it, when it takes longer than
@{tt}per_call_timeout@{utt}, or when its result is too large. With
@{tt}except ToolCallError as e@{utt}, @{tt}e@{utt} is the message text.

@{h2}Answers

A script that ran to the end:

@{code json}
{"status": "ok", "result": {"posted": 42}, "calls": [{"tool": "wf_json_manage_json", "ok": true, "ms": 12}, {"tool": "forum_post_message", "ok": true, "ms": 80}]}
@{body}

@{bullet}@{tt}result@{utt}: the script's @{tt}result@{utt}, or null. Values
JSON does not know come back as their text (a set as
@{tt}"{'a'}"@{utt}).
@{bullet}@{tt}calls@{utt}: every call in order, with its time in
milliseconds.
@{bullet}@{tt}output@{utt}: what the script printed, when it printed.
@{bullet}@{tt}log@{utt}: the log lines, when there are any.

A script that failed -- a Python error, a failed call it did not catch, a
limit, a cancel:

@{code json}
{"status": "error", "error": "KeyError: 'title'", "line": 3, "calls": [{"tool": "wf_json_manage_json", "ok": true, "ms": 9}], "committed_side_effects": "1 tool call(s) committed, 0 failed; nothing after the failure was executed. Committed side effects are NOT rolled back — do not repeat calls marked ok:true.", "variables": {"doc": "d1", "data": "<dict, 5210 chars — omitted>"}}
@{body}

@{bullet}@{tt}error@{utt}: what went wrong, with the exception's type in
front.
@{bullet}@{tt}line@{utt}: the script line the error happened on, also
inside a loop or a function. An abort (cancel, call limit, timeout between
calls) has none.
@{bullet}@{tt}calls@{utt}: as above; a failed call has @{tt}ok: false@{utt}
and its @{tt}error@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}variables@{utt}: the script's variables; values up to 200
characters as they are, larger ones only as type and size.
@{bullet}@{tt}output@{utt} and @{tt}log@{utt}, as above.

Nothing is rolled back: a call marked @{tt}ok: true@{utt} has happened. The
agent sends a new script that goes on after the last such call.

A refusal before anything ran -- no script, a bad timeout, another script
still running in the session -- is only @{tt}{"status": "error", "error":
"..."}@{utt}.

@{h2}dry_run

@{code json}
{"status": "ok", "dry_run": true, "call_tool_targets": ["wf_json_manage_json", "forum_post_message"], "note": "Syntax OK. Sandbox restrictions (no imports, no attribute tricks) are enforced at run time."}
@{body}

Only calls whose tool name is written out as a string are listed. A syntax
error answers @{tt}Syntax error at line N: ...@{utt}.

@{h2}An example

@{code python}
doc = call_tool("wf_json_manage_json", operation="read", doc="d1")
data = parse_json(doc["json"])
posted = []
for item in data["items"]:
    if item["ready"]:
        r = call_tool("forum_post_message", channel_id=5, content=item["text"])
        posted.append(r["message_id"])
log(f"posted {len(posted)}")
result = {"posted": posted}
@{body}
@endnode

@node calls "What a script may call"
@{h1}What a script may call

@{h2}The agent's own tools, and no others

@{tt}call_tool@{utt} hands every call to the agent that runs the script, and
the agent checks it the way it builds the tool list its model sees: the
agent's @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt} first, then @{tt}tools.blocked@{utt}, with
the same pattern matcher. A tool the model does not see, a script cannot
call; an agent without an allowlist can call nothing. The answer is a
ToolCallError such as @{tt}forum_post -> Tool 'forum_post' is not in this
agent's allowed tools.@{utt}

Never callable from a script:
@{bullet}any @{tt}..._run_script@{utt} tool -- a script cannot start
another script;
@{bullet}external MCP tools -- in the model's tool list with "_" where the
dot of their real name is (@{tt}search_web_search@{utt} for
@{tt}search.web_search@{utt}). A script's call answers "Tool
'search_web_search' is an external MCP tool — a script cannot call it. Call
it directly instead."; the agent calls them itself.

The plugin's own @{tt}allowed_tools@{utt} and @{tt}blocked_tools@{utt}
(patterns on the tool name, as in a shell) narrow this further. They never
widen it.

@{h2}What happens on a call

@{bullet}Checked in the script: a cancel, the time left, the call
count, the tool name, the plugin's own lists.
@{bullet}The agent's allowlist -- before anything about the tool's
parameters, so a tool the agent may not call answers "not allowed", never
its parameter list.
@{bullet}The parameters must be plain data (text, numbers, true/false,
null, lists, objects with text keys); the server's @{tt}inject_params@{utt}
for this tool are added (see @{" Setting it up " link setup}); then they
are checked against the tool's schema: an unknown or missing one fails at
once, naming the valid ones.
@{bullet}The agent drops any parameter the script passed that starts with
@{tt}_@{utt} or is @{tt}request_id@{utt} / @{tt}requestId@{utt} -- a script
cannot pose as another session or run -- and runs its
@{tt}pre_tool_call@{utt} hooks, with source @{tt}tool_script@{utt}. A hook
may change the call or stop it; stopped, it fails with the hook's text.
@{bullet}The injected parameters join after the hooks, so no hook logs or
shows them.
@{bullet}The tool runs; its @{tt}post_tool_call@{utt} hooks see the result.
A tool that raises comes back as an error result.
@{bullet}The result is passed through JSON: the script only ever holds
plain data.

@{h2}What a called tool receives

Its parameters, plus what the framework adds: @{tt}_session_id@{utt},
@{tt}_user_id@{utt} (when the run has a user), @{tt}_agent_name@{utt},
@{tt}_agent@{utt}, and a request id of its own -- the script's request id with
@{tt}_ts01@{utt}, @{tt}_ts02@{utt}, ... per call -- as
@{tt}_request_id@{utt} and also as @{tt}request_id@{utt} and
@{tt}requestId@{utt}, without an underscore. A tool whose server reports
status gets its own @{tt}_status@{utt} line. It does not get a
@{tt}_cancellation_token@{utt}; a cancel reaches the script between calls
(see @{" Limits " link limits}).
@endnode

@node language "The script language"
@{h1}The script language

Scripts are a subset of Python, run by an interpreter that walks the
syntax tree and allows only what it knows. There is no way to reach a file,
the network or the server's own objects: no import, no
@{tt}open@{utt}, @{tt}eval@{utt} or @{tt}exec@{utt}, and attributes only on
text, lists, dicts and sets (their listed methods) -- anything else,
@{tt}"".__class__@{utt} or @{tt}call_tool.__globals__@{utt}, is refused. The
only way out of the sandbox is @{tt}call_tool@{utt}.

@{h2}What works

@{bullet}Assignments, also @{tt}a, *rest = items@{utt} and
@{tt}+=@{utt}; @{tt}if@{utt}/@{tt}elif@{utt}/@{tt}else@{utt}, @{tt}for@{utt},
@{tt}while@{utt}, @{tt}x if c else y@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}def@{utt} with positional parameters and defaults,
@{tt}return@{utt}, recursion; @{tt}lambda@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}try@{utt}/@{tt}except@{utt}/@{tt}else@{utt}/@{tt}finally@{utt},
@{tt}raise ValueError("...")@{utt}.
@{bullet}Lists, dicts, sets, tuples; list, dict and set comprehensions and
generator expressions; slicing; f-strings with format specs
(@{tt}f"{x:.2f}"@{utt}); @{tt}in@{utt}, @{tt}is@{utt}, chained comparisons.
@{bullet}Methods: text -- @{tt}split join replace strip lstrip rstrip upper
lower find rfind index rindex count startswith endswith partition
rpartition splitlines center ljust rjust zfill title capitalize swapcase
casefold@{utt} and the @{tt}is...@{utt} tests; lists -- @{tt}append extend
insert remove pop clear count index reverse sort copy@{utt}; dicts --
@{tt}get items keys values pop popitem update setdefault clear copy@{utt};
sets -- @{tt}add remove discard pop clear copy update union intersection
difference symmetric_difference issubset issuperset isdisjoint@{utt}.
@{bullet}Functions: @{tt}len range sorted enumerate min max sum abs round
int float str bool list dict set tuple type isinstance print format hex
bin oct chr ord@{utt}, math (@{tt}sqrt floor ceil pow log exp sin cos
...@{utt}) and @{tt}mean median mode stdev@{utt}. @{tt}min@{utt} and
@{tt}max@{utt} take @{tt}key=@{utt} (a @{tt}lambda@{utt}) and
@{tt}default=@{utt}.

@{h2}What does not

@{bullet}@{tt}import@{utt} -- there is no json module; use
@{tt}parse_json@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}break@{utt} and @{tt}continue@{utt}: use a flag, or a
function that returns.
@{bullet}@{tt}del@{utt}, @{tt}with@{utt}, @{tt}class@{utt},
@{tt}assert@{utt}, @{tt}global@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}zip any all map filter reversed@{utt}, @{tt}str.format@{utt}
(use f-strings), @{tt}str.encode@{utt}.
@{bullet}Names starting with @{tt}_@{utt} (@{tt}_@{utt} alone is allowed).
@{bullet}@{tt}while True:@{utt} is refused as written; any other loop is
bounded (see @{" Limits " link limits}).

@{h2}Surprises

@{bullet}Your own functions take positional arguments only:
@{tt}f(1, b=5)@{utt} ignores @{tt}b@{utt} and uses its default.
@{bullet}A function reads the script's variables, but names it assigns are
gone when it returns; a list or dict it changes stays changed.
@{bullet}One exception type per @{tt}except@{utt}: @{tt}except (KeyError,
ValueError):@{utt} catches neither. @{tt}except ValueError@{utt} also
catches RuntimeError.
@{bullet}@{tt}except Exception:@{utt} and a bare @{tt}except:@{utt} catch
errors, but never a cancel, the call limit, the script timeout or the
print/log cap -- those end the script, also when a @{tt}finally:@{utt}
raises something else on the way out.
@{bullet}@{tt}sum()@{utt} adds numbers only: for lists use
@{tt}extend@{utt}, for text @{tt}join@{utt}.
@{bullet}A list or dict that contains itself cannot be printed or turned
into text: it is refused as too large.
@endnode

@node limits "Limits"
@{h1}Limits

@{table}
Limit | Default | What happens
timeout | 120 s | the whole run, loops included; checked at every step of the script and before every call. A call already running finishes or hits per_call_timeout first, and one guarded operation may take about a second, so a run ends at most about per_call_timeout after the timeout
per_call_timeout | 60 s | one call is cancelled and fails with ToolCallError "... timed out after 60s." -- a script can catch that one
max_tool_calls | 20 | the 21st call ends the script: "max_tool_calls (20) exceeded — split the work into multiple scripts."
max_call_result_bytes | 524288 | one call's result, as JSON characters; larger fails with ToolCallError and the advice to store the payload and pass a reference
max_result_chars | 20000 | the script's @{tt}result@{utt}, as JSON characters; larger fails the script. A failure's error text is cut at this length (at least 1000)
max_output_length | 4000 | characters of @{tt}print@{utt} output, and separately of @{tt}log@{utt} lines; past it the script ends, and no except catches that
@{body}

Fixed in the sandbox:

@{bullet}A loop runs at most 100000 times; @{tt}range()@{utt} gives at most
100000 numbers. Past it the loop fails with an error a script can catch.
@{bullet}Arithmetic that would take the server more than milliseconds is
refused at once: numbers over 200000 bits (about 60000 digits) in
@{tt}**@{utt}, @{tt}*@{utt}, @{tt}//@{utt} and @{tt}%@{utt}; a
three-argument @{tt}pow@{utt} with an exponent or modulus over 2048 bits;
sorting more than a million items; @{tt}round(n, -d)@{utt} with
@{tt}d@{utt} over 60000; @{tt}int(text, base)@{utt} over 200000 bits;
@{tt}sum min max set sorted mean median mode stdev@{utt} over about 30
million characters of content -- a thousand copies of a 60000-digit number,
say; plain floats and small numbers never reach it.
@{bullet}A text or list may not grow past about 6.5 million items -- by
@{tt}*@{utt}, @{tt}+@{utt}, @{tt}extend@{utt}, @{tt}ljust@{utt},
@{tt}rjust@{utt}, @{tt}center@{utt}, @{tt}zfill@{utt}, @{tt}replace@{utt},
@{tt}join@{utt}, a format or @{tt}%@{utt} width or precision
(@{tt}f"{x:>200000000}"@{utt}, @{tt}"%*d" % (n, 1)@{utt}), or turning a
big container into text (@{tt}str@{utt}, @{tt}print@{utt}, f-strings,
@{tt}format@{utt}, @{tt}%@{utt}, an error message) -- the size is estimated
before the text is built. Unbounded recursion ends in a RecursionError.
@{bullet}A memory fuse: when the server process grows by more than 1 GB
while the script runs, the script ends ("Memory grew by more than 1024 MB
during the script; aborted"), and no except catches that. It is read every
50 ms.
@{bullet}One script per session at a time. A second one is refused: "Another
script is already running in this session — wait for it to finish (one
script per session)."

A cancel of the run is checked at every step of the script, before every
call and while a call waits; the script then ends with "Request cancelled —
script aborted ...". A tool that is already running is not stopped by it.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The tool description and the parameter texts come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}: when to use a script (two or more dependent calls),
the rules for @{tt}call_tool@{utt}, @{tt}result@{utt},
@{tt}parse_json@{utt} and @{tt}log@{utt}, what the sandbox allows and what
not, and a short example. Everything a failed run answers -- the error, the
calls, the variables -- is made for the model to write the next script
from.

In the chat, the call's status line follows the script: "script: call 2 —
wf_json_manage_json", "script: <a log line>", and at the end "Script ran:
2/2 tool call(s)". A failed run ends the line with its error.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

This is what the plugin is for. The model pays for the script it writes and
for the answer -- the @{tt}result@{utt}, the list of calls, what was printed
and logged -- but not for the results of the calls in between, which
never leave the server, and it types no value a second time. Four
dependent calls are one model turn instead of four.

The answer is an ordinary tool result: appended, nothing earlier changes,
the cached prompt stays as it was. Keep @{tt}result@{utt} small; a large
payload belongs in a store, with its reference in the result.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}The memory fuse measures the whole server process: other work
growing at the same time counts against the script, and a loop that
allocates fast can pass the 1 GB by a few hundred MB before the next
reading. Without psutil there is no fuse.
@{bullet}A script that only computes keeps a worker thread busy up to its
timeout.
@{bullet}External MCP tools cannot be called from a script.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The default configuration has no tool_script server. The reference agent,
@{tt}workflow_agent@{utt}, brings its own in its agent file
(@{tt}config/agents/workflow_agent.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    wf_pipe:
      type: tool_script
      enabled: true
      description: "workflow_agent scripted tool chains"
      config:
        timeout: 120
        per_call_timeout: 60
        max_tool_calls: 20
@{body}

An agent gets the tool, and the tools its scripts may call, through its
allowlist:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "wf_json/*"
      - "wf_pipe/*"
      - "datetime/*"
@{body}

Here scripts can call the @{tt}wf_json@{utt} and @{tt}datetime@{utt} tools
-- and nothing else, whatever the script says. The plugin needs
@{tt}jsonschema@{utt} and the @{tt}script_interpreter@{utt} plugin's
sandbox in the tree; it keeps no data.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
timeout | 120 | seconds for a whole run; the model may ask for less
per_call_timeout | 60 | seconds for one call
max_tool_calls | 20 | calls per script
max_call_result_bytes | 524288 | largest result of one call, in JSON characters
max_result_chars | 20000 | largest @{tt}result@{utt}, in JSON characters
max_output_length | 4000 | characters of print output, and of log lines
allowed_tools | none | patterns on tool names a script may call; empty: whatever the agent may call
blocked_tools | none | patterns on tool names a script may not call
inject_params | none | values added to calls, see below
@{body}

@{h2}inject_params

Values the server adds to a script's calls, for secrets the model must
never type: a mapping from a tool-name pattern to parameters.

@{code yaml}
      config:
        inject_params:
          "records_update": {write_key: "..."}
@{body}

The configured value always wins over one the script passed, and the script
may leave the parameter out even when the tool requires it. The values join
after the tool hooks, so no hook logs them; a parameter starting with
@{tt}_@{utt} or named @{tt}request_id@{utt}/@{tt}requestId@{utt} is dropped.
An entry that is not a mapping is ignored with a warning in the log.
@endnode
